    <exercises xml:id="exercises-angle-between-two-lines">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-angles-in-a-cuboid">
            <statement><p>A cuboid <m>ABCDEFGH</m> has <m>AB = 14\,\text{cm}</m>, <m>BC = 8\,\text{cm}</m> and <m>AE = 5\,\text{cm}</m>. Find (a) angle <m>GAB</m>; (b) angle <m>CAH</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>(a) <m>34.0^{\circ}</m> (b) <m>65.1^{\circ}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>(a) Angle <m>ABG = 90^{\circ}</m>. <m>AG = \sqrt{196 + 64 + 25} = \sqrt{285}</m>, so <m>\cos GAB = \dfrac{14}{\sqrt{285}}</m> and angle <m>GAB \approx 34.0^{\circ}</m>.</p>
            <p>(b) Triangle <m>ACH</m>: <m>AC^2 = 196 + 64 = 260</m>, <m>AH^2 = 64 + 25 = 89</m>, <m>CH^2 = 196 + 25 = 221</m>. No right angle, so <m>\cos CAH = \dfrac{260 + 89 - 221}{2\sqrt{260}\sqrt{89}}</m> and angle <m>CAH \approx 65.1^{\circ}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-angle-in-a-tent">
            <statement><p>For the tent in <xref ref="fig-tent-prism"/> (<m>AB = 2.4\,\text{m}</m>, <m>CA = CB = 1.5\,\text{m}</m>, <m>AD = 3\,\text{m}</m>), find angle <m>DAF</m>, between the bottom edge <m>AD</m> and the line <m>AF</m> from a front corner to the back of the ridge.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>26.6^{\circ}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>AD</m> is at right angles to the back end <m>DEF</m>, which contains <m>DF</m>, so angle <m>ADF = 90^{\circ}</m>. <m>DF = 1.5\,\text{m}</m> and <m>AD = 3\,\text{m}</m>, so <m>\tan DAF = \dfrac{1.5}{3}</m> and angle <m>DAF \approx 26.6^{\circ}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-angles-of-a-rectangular-pyramid">
            <statement><p>A right pyramid <m>VABCD</m> has a rectangular base with <m>AB = 16\,\text{cm}</m> and <m>BC = 12\,\text{cm}</m>. Each sloping edge is <m>14\,\text{cm}</m> long. Find angles <m>AVB</m> and <m>BVC</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>Angle <m>AVB \approx 69.7^{\circ}</m>; angle <m>BVC \approx 50.8^{\circ}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>Triangle <m>AVB</m>: <m>\cos AVB = \dfrac{196 + 196 - 256}{2 \times 196} = \dfrac{136}{392}</m>, so angle <m>AVB \approx 69.7^{\circ}</m>. Triangle <m>BVC</m>: <m>\cos BVC = \dfrac{196 + 196 - 144}{392} = \dfrac{248}{392}</m>, so angle <m>BVC \approx 50.8^{\circ}</m>. The longer base edge is opposite the larger angle.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-corner-pyramid-angle">
            <statement><p>A pyramid <m>VABC</m> has a right angle <m>BAC</m> in its base, <m>AB = AC = 10\,\text{cm}</m>, and the edge <m>VA = 7\,\text{cm}</m> is vertical. Find angle <m>BVC</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>70.8^{\circ}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>VB = VC = \sqrt{100 + 49} = \sqrt{149}</m> and <m>BC = \sqrt{100 + 100} = \sqrt{200}</m>. By the cosine rule, <m>\cos BVC = \dfrac{149 + 149 - 200}{2 \times 149} = \dfrac{98}{298}</m>, so angle <m>BVC \approx 70.8^{\circ}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-grain-store-wires">
            <statement><p>A grain store at a cereals depot in Eldoret is a cuboid <m>ABCDEFGH</m> with <m>AB = 15\,\text{m}</m>, <m>BC = 9\,\text{m}</m> and <m>AE = 5\,\text{m}</m>. Wires run from the ceiling corner <m>G</m> to the floor corners <m>A</m> and <m>C</m>. Find the angle <m>AGC</m> between the wires.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>74.0^{\circ}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>GC</m> is vertical, so triangle <m>AGC</m> has a right angle at <m>C</m>. <m>AC = \sqrt{225 + 81} = \sqrt{306}</m> and <m>GC = 5</m>, so <m>\tan AGC = \dfrac{\sqrt{306}}{5}</m> and angle <m>AGC \approx 74.0^{\circ}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-angle-between-space-diagonals">
            <title>Challenge: Two Space Diagonals of a Cube</title>
            <statement><p>Show that the angle between two space diagonals of a cube, <m>AG</m> and <m>CE</m>, is about <m>70.5^{\circ}</m>, whatever the size of the cube.</p></statement>
            <hint><p>The diagonals cross at the centre <m>X</m> of the cube. Use the rectangle <m>ACGE</m> and the triangle <m>AXC</m>.</p></hint>
            <answer><p>Angle <m>AXC \approx 109.5^{\circ}</m>, so the angle between the lines is <m>180^{\circ} - 109.5^{\circ} = 70.5^{\circ}</m>.</p></answer>
            <solution><p>Let the edge be <m>a</m>. Then <m>AC = a\sqrt{2}</m> and <m>AG = CE = a\sqrt{3}</m>. The diagonals bisect each other at <m>X</m>, so <m>XA = XC = \frac{a\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>. In triangle <m>AXC</m>:
            <md>\cos AXC = \frac{\frac{3a^2}{4} + \frac{3a^2}{4} - 2a^2}{2 \times \frac{3a^2}{4}} = \frac{-\frac{a^2}{2}}{\frac{3a^2}{2}} = -\frac{1}{3}</md>
            The <m>a^2</m> cancels, so the angle does not depend on the size of the cube. Angle <m>AXC \approx 109.5^{\circ}</m> is obtuse, so the angle between the two lines is <m>180^{\circ} - 109.5^{\circ} = 70.5^{\circ}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-angle-between-two-lines">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Work through activity part (b) with the model in hand and <xref ref="example-angle-in-a-right-angled-triangle"/>. Give a <q>triangle finder</q> card: where do the lines meet; where are their other ends; is there a right angle? Assign <xref ref="ex-angles-in-a-cuboid"/> (a) and <xref ref="ex-angle-in-a-tent"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity plus all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-angles-in-a-cuboid"/> to <xref ref="ex-grain-store-wires"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-angle-between-space-diagonals"/>. Extension: for the tent in <xref ref="fig-tent-prism"/>, find the angle between the skew lines <m>AB</m> and <m>CE</m>. (Answer: <m>AB</m> is parallel to <m>DE</m>, which meets <m>CE</m> at <m>E</m>. Triangle <m>CDE</m> has <m>DE = 2.4\,\text{m}</m> and <m>CD = CE = \sqrt{1.5^2 + 3^2} = \sqrt{11.25}</m> m, so <m>\cos CED = \dfrac{1.2}{\sqrt{11.25}}</m> and the angle is about <m>69.0^{\circ}</m>.)</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

